# WALKING ACROSS THE DOORSTEP — plan 22 `D1`.
#
# ⛔ **THIS SCRIPT EXISTS BECAUSE A SABOTAGE FOUND NOTHING.** `D1` reads the derived
# mode in two places — after every script line, and inside every tick — and
# `probe/k2/sabotage-mode.sh` disabled each in turn against the whole corpus. The
# per-line call moved one record. **The per-tick call moved NONE**: every mode
# crossing in the thirty scripts is a TELEPORT, so the branch written for a person
# walking into a building was correct and observed by nothing, which is this tree's
# commonest defect wearing its usual clothes.
#
# So this is the walk. No `snap`, no `frame`, no camera: what it is for is the pair of
# `mode` lines in `probe/k3d`'s record, printed from inside `run_ticks` and from
# nowhere else — the `notick` row of `sabotage-mode` is red on this script alone.
#
# ⚠ **A DECK, NOT A HOUSE, AND THAT WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN PREFERRED.** Walking at a
# house stops the feet at `0,-2.26` — its own near wall — and cutting an opening in
# that wall first does not let the walker through either. A storey has no walls, so the
# only thing between the author and the shelter is distance, which is what a threshold
# has to be for this to be testing a WALK.
#
# ⚠ IT WALKS BOTH WAYS ON PURPOSE. A guard that is right entering and blind leaving is
# a bug this tree has now written twice (`faced_between`, `stroke_over_limit`), and the
# only shape that can see it is the incremental one — which is what a walk is.

echo --- ground to stand on
# ⚠ THE RAISE LANDS AHEAD OF THE AUTHOR, NOT UNDER THEM — `raise_ahead` puts its dome
# ten hexes along the facing with radius 7, so a `verb storey` pressed here is refused
# with *no cells here* at `GROUND=0`. Measured, then the pose moved onto the dome.
at 0 0 0
verb raise
wait rebuilt

echo --- a deck over the middle of it
at 17 0 90
verb storey
wait rebuilt

echo --- out from under it, into the open
at 17 -8 90
feet

echo --- and walk back under, stopping beneath it
# ⚠ 75 TICKS IS MEASURED, NOT ROUND: this walk covers 0.1067 wu a tick, so 200 carried
# the author straight through the disc and out the far side — two crossings in one
# direction, which is a weaker row than one crossing in each.
keys 1
step 75
keys 0
feet

echo --- and out again, WALKING BACKWARD along the same facing
# ⚠ `turn 180` is refused by this runner — it is a feedback loop on a live pose and
# needs a server to read back from. `keys 2` is S, which walks the same line the other
# way and keeps the crossing incremental, which is the whole point of the row.
keys 2
step 75
keys 0
feet
